"""Targeted third-review repairs from an immutable pre-repair snapshot.

Run after earlier repair scripts, never before them. No network or model calls.
"""
import copy
import hashlib
import json
import re
import shutil
import sys
from pathlib import Path

OUT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
WS=OUT.parents[2]
REPO=WS.parents[1]
sys.path.insert(0,str(REPO/'standard/v0.2.0-draft/checks'))
from yyeni_checks import _artefact_text

def read(p):return json.loads(p.read_text())
def dump(p,x):p.parent.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True);p.write_text(json.dumps(x,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\n')
def snapshot(p):
    b=OUT/'baseline'/p.relative_to(WS)
    if not b.exists():b.parent.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True);shutil.copy2(p,b)
    return read(b)

TOPICS={}
for td in sorted(p for p in (WS/'topics').iterdir() if (p/'contract.json').exists()):
    ip=next((td/'learning-items').glob('topic_*_items.json'))
    lp=td/'claims/canonical_claim_ledger.json'
    TOPICS[td.name]={'td':td,'ip':ip,'lp':lp,'items':snapshot(ip),'ledger':snapshot(lp),
        'units':{p.name:snapshot(p) for p in sorted((td/'content-units').glob('*.json'))},
        'notes':snapshot(td/'authoring_notes.json')}

def item(t,n):return next(i for i in TOPICS[t]['items']['items'] if i['item_id'].split('-')[3]==str(n).zfill(3))
def unit(t,n):return TOPICS[t]['units'][f'CU-6136-{t}.{n:02d}.json']
def block(t,n,ending):return next(b for b in unit(t,n)['blocks'] if b['block_id'].endswith('-'+ending))

# R3-01: each four-mark explanation develops the supplied context through four
# linked contributions, rather than treating answer length as a universal rubric.
EXPLAIN={
'5.1':'The group already has stored crops and willing buyers, so a shortage of seed is not the immediate sales constraint. The blocked route prevents delivery, leaving existing output unsold. A seed loan would add repayment obligations and may add crops that still cannot reach buyers. A feasible delivery or access arrangement should therefore be investigated first; its cost and reliability must be checked before assuming sales will rise.',
'6.1':'New paid work can increase money income, but counting jobs does not show the amount or reliability of those earnings. Losing growing land removes a source of household food and may reduce women’s control over productive resources. Purchasing replacement food can absorb some of the new income, while paid work may add to an unchanged care workload. Compare net resources, food access, time and decision-making control before judging whether overall welfare and choice improved.',
'6.2':'Equal admission removes a formal rule excluding otherwise eligible participants. It does not provide an affordable journey to the training site, so people without transport may still be absent. A timetable that participants cannot attend creates a separate barrier even if a journey becomes affordable. Actual access therefore needs suitable travel and scheduling arrangements, with attendance and completion checked across groups; equal written rules alone do not establish equal opportunities in practice.',
'7.1':'Mean density divides each district’s population by its area, so equal means can conceal different settlement patterns. A clinic near a single cluster may be within reach of many residents in the first district. In the dispersed district, the same central-site approach can leave small villages with long journeys even though average density is identical. Locate services using settlement positions and travel evidence; distributed visits or outreach may complement fixed clinics where resources and safe access allow.',
'7.2':'The new building supplies space but cannot examine or treat patients without trained staff. Without a funded medicine supply, it also cannot provide treatments that depend on those medicines. Unaffordable travel can prevent residents reaching the clinic even after staffing and supplies improve. The programme therefore needs an operating budget and practical access arrangements alongside construction; assess care actually delivered and who can use it rather than counting buildings alone.',
'7.3':'Working adults may be unable to attend daytime lessons because attendance conflicts with their paid work. Evening provision removes that stated time conflict, though its actual travel and safety arrangements still need checking. Available tools allow learners to practise the skills rather than receive theory alone. Equal admission rules do not supply either usable time or practical equipment, so judge effective access and learning opportunities from these delivery conditions as well as formal eligibility.',
'8.1':'Transferring tasks gives the local body responsibility for water services and may bring decisions closer to users. It does not itself create trained operators, so maintenance and reliable operation can remain impossible. Missing funds separately prevent the purchase of supplies and payment for necessary work. Transfer should therefore be accompanied by defined resources, skills and accountability; measure reliable access for residents rather than assuming that a change of responsibility improves services.',
'9.1':'Export receipts equal quantity sold multiplied by the unit price, so a lower price reduces receipts even when export quantity is unchanged. Essential imports still require the same payment under the stated fixed costs. Export receipts therefore cover less of those import payments, increasing the need for other foreign receipts, finance or adjustment. Investment could help finance a payment gap, but it is a different transaction and does not itself improve the goods trade balance or remove future financing risks.',
'9.2':'A machine that remains unused cannot supply the output or earnings expected to justify its cost. Loan repayments still fall due, committing resources without that productive return. Smaller assistance addressing the working producer’s missing input can enable existing labour and equipment to produce, if demand and operating conditions support it. Compare usable output, retained benefits and repayment obligations; a larger headline loan amount does not by itself establish a better development result.',
'9.3':'Tariff cooperation can reduce the charges on qualifying regional goods, making exchange more attractive. The broken physical connection still prevents those goods reaching buyers, so lower charges alone cannot create the intended deliveries. Without deliveries, producers cannot realise the expected market access or sales gains from the agreement. A feasible maintained transport connection and workable border implementation must complement the tariff arrangement; judge actual participation and delivered trade rather than agreement signing alone.'
}
for t,text in EXPLAIN.items():
    i=item(t,'P01');a=i['canonical_answer'].split('(b)',1)[0]
    i['canonical_answer']=a+'(b) '+text
    i['marking_guidance']=[g for g in i['marking_guidance'] if not g.startswith('Development (b)')]
    i['marking_guidance'].insert(1,'Development (b), up to 4 marks: develop the supplied barrier through its direct effect, a further consequence and the implication for the stated judgement or response. Credit distinct accurate links in context; repeated assertions or unsupported new facts do not add development. The canonical answer models a developed chain, not a compulsory wording or one-mark-per-sentence rule.')

# R3-02: authored worked prose with different cases or quantities. No card prompt
# is removed from a teaching paragraph by a global answer substitution.
WORKED={
'5.1.02':'In a fictional farming community, a planting calendar reserves a shared plough for the first suitable rains. If bookings repeatedly overlap, some plots are planted late even though land and seed are available. A transparent schedule and a backup arrangement may reduce this coordination problem. The season matters, but its effect also depends on how people organise equipment and labour.',
'5.1.03':'A fictional commercial farm grows vegetables for one processor, while a neighbouring farm supplies vegetables, eggs and fruit to several buyers. The second has more varied sources of revenue and may be less exposed to one product’s price fall. It also needs skills and equipment for several activities. Diversification spreads some risks; it does not remove weather, finance or market constraints.',
'5.1.04':'An invented cooperative can either fund shared grading equipment or offer members a larger fertiliser loan. Buyers already accept the crop, but inconsistent grading causes price deductions. Grading equipment addresses that observed marketing problem; fertiliser could raise production but does not correct the deductions. Compare the equipment’s maintenance costs and the expected avoided losses before choosing. A scheme should fit the diagnosed constraint.',
'5.1.05':'In a fictional garden scheme, covering an open storage tank reduces evaporation from 18 to 8 units out of 100 stored, leaving 92 rather than 82 units available before other losses. That is ten extra units from the same initial supply. Benefits depend on a suitable cover, maintenance and fair access arrangements; a water-saving installation alone does not settle who receives the saved water.',
'5.1.06':'Residents in a fictional village agree published opening times for a shared store. A recognised committee keeps stock records, a trained operator manages daily work, and members receive regular accounts. These roles complement each other: assigning an operator does not replace oversight, and oversight does not supply missing skills. Responsibilities should identify who acts, who checks and how users can raise a problem.',
'5.1.07':'A fictional guesthouse can buy meals from a village group or import prepared meals. Local purchasing may retain income and support skills, but the group must meet agreed food standards and delivery times. Imported meals may be easier to arrange initially while offering fewer local supplier links. Compare feasibility, working conditions and retained spending; visitor numbers alone do not measure community benefit.',
'5.1.08':'In an invented comparison, Visitor Scheme C retains N$45 locally from each N$100 spent and uses 25 litres of water per visitor. Scheme D retains N$30 and uses 40 litres. C retains N$15 more and uses 15 litres less per visitor. These measures favour C on those two criteria, but employment quality, conservation and resident participation still need separate evidence.',
'6.1.01':'A household food diary in a fictional settlement records vegetables grown for consumption, meals prepared and hours of care alongside cash earnings. Selling no produce does not make the vegetables valueless: they meet needs that would otherwise require purchases. Care also supports other members’ participation. Assess the contribution without assuming that the person doing the work necessarily controls the household’s income.',
'6.1.02':'In a fictional savings group, important decisions are made while some members are collecting children from school. Written membership rights do not give those members an effective voice at that time. Consultation over meeting times and a way to submit views can improve participation. Such arrangements should respond to the actual members’ duties rather than assume that all women share the same responsibilities.',
'6.1.03':'In a fictional historical comparison, a household gains wage income after moving closer to an employer, but now buys food formerly grown on its own plot. The wage gain must be compared with replacement food costs and changed work and care time. Employment can expand opportunities while another change reduces control over resources; neither wage access nor the loss alone establishes the combined welfare result.',
'6.2.01':'A fictional skills centre allows all qualified applicants to enrol but provides instructions only in a language some applicants cannot use. Equal eligibility is a formal opportunity; understandable information is part of practical access. Suitable communication and feedback may widen participation. Check whether the relevant groups actually enrol and complete the programme rather than assuming that a rule change produces equal outcomes.',
'6.2.02':'After a fictional programme introduces accessible information and revises its timetable, attendance rises from 35% to 60%, an increase of 25 percentage points. The pattern is consistent with improved access but does not isolate which change caused it. Separate evidence on who attends, who completes and what barriers remain is needed to assess the programme’s effects.',
'6.2.03':'An invented council invites women’s associations to submit written priorities before budget decisions. Written submissions reduce some travel demands, but people without literacy support may still be excluded. Facilitated local discussions can collect those views while keeping the final decision process open. Count influence on actual priorities as well as the number of invitations; participation needs a usable route to decisions.',
'6.2.04':'A fictional enterprise course can offer an accessible local venue for fewer people or a large central session with no travel assistance. The central session reaches more places on paper, while the local venue may be usable by residents who cannot afford the journey. Compare completion, quality and access for the intended group; a lower attendance total does not by itself establish a weaker inclusive result.',
'6.2.05':'A fictional information service receives few enquiries because callers expect their names to become public. Clear confidentiality arrangements, trained staff and a safe way to make first contact can reduce this barrier. Low use does not establish low need. Information about violence or child safety should focus on non-blaming support and competent services rather than require learners to disclose personal experiences.',
'6.2.06':'In a fictional support programme, a nearby trained adviser offers confidential appointments and explains referral options. This may help people seek assistance earlier, but consent, privacy and continued service capacity still matter. Prevention and support need complementary actions; telling people to seek help is insufficient if the route is unsafe, unavailable or unaffordable.',
'7.1.01':'A fictional district with 18,000 residents across 600 square kilometres has a mean density of 30 people per square kilometre. That calculation says nothing about whether the residents cluster around one town or live in scattered villages. A settlement map supplies distribution evidence, while journey information helps judge actual access to services.',
'7.1.02':'On an invented settlement map, six of nine settlements are near a railway and its service centres; three lie farther away. This is a concentration associated with transport and service access. It does not prove why each settlement formed. Historical activity, available water and land conditions may also help explain the pattern and should be investigated separately.',
'7.1.03':'Two invented populations change over the same five-year interval: C rises from 200,000 to 210,000, while D rises from 50,000 to 60,000. Both gain 10,000 people, but the percentage increases are 5% and 20%. Comparing size, absolute increase and percentage growth answers different questions; state which measure the judgement uses.',
'7.1.04':'A fictional population programme combines confidential voluntary services with measures that keep learners in education. The service can address an immediate information or access need, while education may widen longer-term choices. Their effects occur through different pathways and need evidence over suitable periods. Respect for rights and genuine access are conditions of the programme, not optional costs to be traded away.',
'7.1.05':'A fictional district expects 360 children to need primary places and currently has 240 usable places, a gap of 120. A neighbouring district expects 180 children and has 200 places. The first has the larger capacity gap, but staff, classroom condition and journey distances still matter. A total population count alone would not reveal this age-specific need.',
'7.1.06':'An original age-structure comparison gives one population 45% below age 15 and 5% aged 65 or over; another has 25% and 15% respectively. The first may face greater school-capacity demands relative to population; the second may need more older-age services. These are planning implications of the shares, not proof that every member of an age group is dependent or has the same needs.',
'7.1.07':'In a fictional service review, continuity of HIV care improves while reported illness-related absence declines. This association can inform investigation of treatment access, but changes in recording or workplace conditions may also matter. Population and employment effects require consistent denominators and dates. People living with HIV should not be treated as a uniform group or excluded on assumptions about their capability.',
'7.2.01':'In an invented settlement, a reliable protected water supply is paired with maintained toilets and handwashing facilities. These measures can reduce exposure to some water-borne infections through different routes. They do not prevent every disease: malaria, for example, has a mosquito transmission route. Match the prevention measure to the route and check whether the facilities remain usable.',
'7.2.02':'A fictional health budget can expand a staffed mobile service or equip a fixed centre for more specialised care. Outreach can reduce journeys for routine needs; a fixed centre may handle cases needing equipment that cannot travel. Neither replaces an effective referral route. Priorities depend on the pattern of need, coverage and operating capacity, with benefits and limitations weighed for both choices.',
'7.2.03':'A fictional household loses paid hours during a period of illness and spends savings on care and transport. A second adult’s caring responsibilities can reduce earnings further. Accessible professional services and income support may reduce some pressures, but the particular household’s circumstances matter. Do not infer an individual’s health or ability to work from an HIV diagnosis alone.',
'7.3.01':'A recognised school course can teach inherited farming knowledge, so traditional content does not automatically make the organisation non-formal. Conversely, a community-run literacy course can be planned and systematic while operating outside the main formal system. Distinguish what is taught from how learning is organised, and apply each classification to the stated facts.',
'7.3.03':'A fictional adult literacy group meets at a community hall on two agreed mornings each week, uses a planned course and checks progress. It is organised learning outside the main school system, illustrating non-formal education. A conversation that happens to teach someone a new word is informal learning instead. A planned setting and purpose distinguish the two.',
'7.3.04':'In an invented comparison, one district enrols 180 of 300 eligible children and another enrols 160 of 200. Their enrolment rates are 60% and 80%; the first has more enrolled children but a lower share of its eligible group. Enrolment also differs from regular attendance, completion and learning quality, which require separate measures.',
'7.3.05':'An invented learning centre supplies offline audio lessons with charged players and a tutor trained to support their use. The equipment can widen access to materials, but lessons still need to suit the curriculum and learners’ language needs. A device count is an input measure; participation and learning provide better evidence of whether the arrangement is useful.',
'7.3.06':'A fictional tailoring course has trained instructors, appropriate machines and local employers willing to assess applicants. Practical work can develop useful skills, while classroom explanations help learners understand methods. Employment still depends on opportunities and the quality and recognition of the training. A vocational label alone does not guarantee either competence or a job.',
'7.3.07':'An invented school introduces accurate HIV education and a confidential route for reporting discrimination. Explaining that ordinary classroom contact does not transmit HIV can address a false belief, while staff action is needed when exclusion continues. Information and supportive behaviour reinforce each other; learners should not be required to identify anyone’s health status.',
'8.1.01':'An elected fictional committee publishes its budget, records reasons for major decisions and allows residents to question spending. Elections give members a mandate, while reporting and scrutiny help make their exercise of power accountable between elections. Representation does not remove the need to explain decisions or provide a route for concerns.',
'8.1.02':'A fictional state can have a hereditary head of state while elected representatives make ordinary laws under a constitution. The succession arrangement describes monarchy; it does not by itself tell us how much democratic control citizens exercise. Classification needs separate evidence about selection of rulers, limits on power and public participation.',
'8.1.03':'In an invented economy, privately owned farms sell produce while a publicly owned enterprise operates electricity networks. Ownership is mixed across activities. This differs from a claim that all production is privately owned, and says nothing by itself about service quality or the distribution of benefits. Use the actual ownership arrangements before judging outcomes.',
'8.1.04':'A fictional local authority receives maintenance responsibilities together with a funded budget, trained operators and public reporting requirements. Local knowledge can help identify recurring faults, while resources make a response possible. If the budget is repeatedly delayed, the service can still fail despite the transfer. Distinguish responsibility, capacity and accountability when assessing decentralisation.',
'8.1.05':'An invented training facility has equipment and power, but purchases repeatedly bypass transparent procedures. Funds may buy less useful provision if decisions favour private interests instead of the stated need. Published procurement rules, competent oversight and usable complaints routes can reduce this risk; a rule’s existence alone does not establish that it is enforced.',
'9.1.01':'A fictional pottery group sells surplus goods to another district and buys packaging it cannot produce efficiently itself. Exchange can widen its market and supply useful inputs, provided transport costs and product quality permit viable trade. The benefit comes from the goods and opportunities obtained, not simply from money changing hands.',
'9.1.02':'Two fictional producers specialise in different products suited to their skills and resources, then exchange part of their output. Specialisation may improve productivity and widen the range available to each. Dependence on a single customer or an unreliable supplier can limit those gains. Assess the terms and reliability of exchange alongside the productive opportunity.',
'9.1.03':'A fictional country records annual goods exports of N$75 million and goods imports of N$68 million. Exports minus imports gives a goods trade surplus of N$7 million. This balance excludes services and financial transactions, so it is not a complete measure of external payments or development. A separate investment inflow must not be added as if it were a goods export.',
'9.1.04':'A fictional exporter sells 500 units at N$20 each, then the same quantity at N$16. Receipts fall from N$10,000 to N$8,000. If its foreign payment commitments do not change, fewer export receipts are available to meet them. This illustrates price exposure; a real judgement would also examine other receipts, finance and the possibility of adjustment.',
'9.1.05':'In an invented export table, Country C receives 65% of export earnings from primary goods and 35% from manufactures. Country D receives 40% and 60%. D has the larger manufactured share; C may be more exposed to its primary-export markets. Neither table establishes income distribution, every product traded or the permanent development category of a country.',
'9.1.06':'An invented design business shares plans with a foreign workshop and coordinates orders across several countries. These links illustrate globalisation through connected production, communication and exchange. They are conceptually different from a change in global average temperature. A cross-border link does not itself establish the scale or distribution of local development benefits.',
'9.1.07':'A fictional processing agreement offers local technicians supervised training and includes purchases from nearby suppliers. Those links can retain skills and spending beyond immediate output. They require workable training, suitable supplier quality and fair work conditions. Assess actual delivery, costs and retained benefits; ownership or a foreign connection alone does not establish the result.',
'9.1.08':'An invented exporter has customers and transport but its containers fail the buyers’ agreed labelling requirements. Feasible quality-control training addresses the immediate access constraint more directly than extra advertising. The producer must still meet cost and delivery requirements. International exposure is useful when it leads to capabilities that fit the actual market opportunity.',
'9.2.01':'A fictional school has trained staff but needs technical design and funding for a safe sanitation system. Suitable assistance should match both gaps and include a credible operating and maintenance arrangement. A grant avoids repayment, but its size alone does not establish useful delivery. Recipient priorities and the terms of support affect whether the result can last.',
'9.2.02':'An invented council compares a small grant for a limited service with an affordable loan that reaches more residents but commits later revenue. The grant protects future funds; the loan widens current reach. Neither financing label settles the judgement. Compare need, maintained operation, repayment capacity and the services postponed under each feasible option.',
'9.2.03':'A fictional programme seeking child-focused nutrition support needs a different institutional role from one seeking finance for an external-payment shortfall. Match the agency’s mandate and type of assistance to the actual problem before evaluating terms and delivery. A broad international role does not mean an organisation performs every service or supplies unconditional funds.',
'9.3.01':'A fictional regional agreement recognises shared product-testing results, reducing repeated testing for qualifying goods. Producers still need compliant products and workable implementation at borders. The agreement creates an opportunity to reduce costs, while unequal testing capacity can leave some firms unable to benefit. Assess practical participation and costs rather than equate cooperation with identical gains for all members.',
'9.3.02':'An invented small producer receives information about an external-market agreement but lacks a reliable product-testing process. Suitable technical support can help it meet the applicable requirements; market access on paper does not guarantee orders. Compare compliance costs, viable buyers and locally retained benefits before judging the cooperation’s contribution.',
'10.1.01':'With permission to observe a safe public site for one hour on three days, a fictional research group narrows its topic to the number of buses arriving in each equal interval. It does not claim to study private reasons for travel because it cannot interview passengers. The question fits what can be safely observed within the available time.',
'10.1.02':'A fictional learner is interested in employment but cannot safely contact employers or collect private earnings records. A narrower enquiry into the opening times of accessible public training services may be feasible and relevant to development. Interest helps sustain the work; relevance, ethical access and a clear scope determine whether usable evidence can answer the question.',
'10.1.03':'An invented enquiry asks whether waiting times differ between two authorised service points. Its aim is to investigate that comparison. Specific objectives are to record waiting minutes over the same agreed period and compare the resulting distributions. The objectives guide collection; a proposal to change staffing would follow the findings rather than stand in for them.',
'10.1.04':'For an invented study, the question is whether sites farther from a collection depot have more uncollected containers during the same week. A testable hypothesis predicts that association. Distance and counts must be defined and collected consistently. An observed association could support the hypothesis without proving distance caused the missed collections.',
'10.1.05':'A fictional group records its own queue counts for its present enquiry: these are primary data. It also uses a previously published service timetable: that is secondary data for this enquiry. Either can be numerical, printed or digital. Origin determines the classification; suitability and accuracy still require checks on definitions, dates and coverage.',
'10.1.06':'A fictional travel survey asks consenting adults for minutes spent on a specified journey, including waiting, using the same neutral questions for each respondent. A separate observation sheet records visible queue length at agreed times. The questionnaire supplies reported experience, while observation supplies visible counts; the researchers should not silently substitute one measure for the other.',
'10.1.07':'An invented enquiry needs to know when a public service opens and why its hours change. Repeated safe observation records the visible opening times more directly than people’s recollections. A consenting provider interview can explain the arrangements, but its account should not be treated as an observed timetable. Different questions can justify complementary methods.',
'10.1.08':'A fictional pilot finds that respondents interpret “frequent” visits differently. The researcher revises the question to ask for a count during the past seven days, checks that participants understand the period, then tries the revised wording again. The pilot identifies a measurement problem; its few answers do not establish the population’s actual pattern.',
'10.1.09':'An invented study defines its population as households listed in two named settlements during a specified month. A sample selects some of those households for observation or voluntary questions. It cannot automatically represent households outside that area or later residents. A clear population definition makes it possible to assess what the sampling frame includes or misses.',
'10.1.10':'For an original stratified example, a list contains 60 households in one area and 30 in another. A proportional sample of 18 allocates 12 places and six places respectively, followed by random selection within each group. The design preserves planned group proportions. Incomplete lists and refusals still need recording; proportional allocation does not remove every source of bias.',
'10.1.11':'An original observation table records 3, 6 and 5 users in three named waiting-time categories. A bar graph gives the categories on the horizontal axis and users on a labelled vertical scale from zero; separated equal-width bars show heights 3, 6 and 5. A title, time period and source identify what is represented. The middle category is largest, but the graph alone does not explain why.',
'10.1.12':'In an invented enquiry, repeated records show that a service opens late because a qualified operator arrives after the scheduled start. A feasible recommendation could revise staffing arrangements through the responsible provider. It addresses the observed obstacle more directly than an unrelated poster. State the evidence, responsible actor and resources, and distinguish a proposed response from one already carried out.'
}
